  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\n...TONIGHT AND THIS WEEKEND...\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and upper air analysis this afternoon \nshows a sharp and vigorous deep layer trough progressing eastward \nout of the central CONUS, a weak upper low southwest of southern \nCalifornia, and a building upper ridge across the Intermountain \nWest. At the surface, a potent post-frontal airmass continues to \ndominate the central plains with gusty northern winds supporting \ncontinue strong low-level CAA. However, as a surface ridge slides \ninto Kansas tonight, winds are expected to quickly decrease become \nnearly calm by late tonight. These calm winds, along with clear \nskies, should allow for ample radiational cooling. It's likely much \nof the area will see their first frost and/or freeze of the season, \nespecially across central Kansas. With dewpoints in the upper 20s, \nand such optimal conditions for radiational cooling, one would think \nthat the bottom may fall out on min temps overnight; however, soil \ntemps in the 50s and 60s should somewhat limit how cold conditions \nget overnight/early Saturday morning. Still lows will generally \nrange from the upper 20s across central Kansas to the mid 30s across \nsoutheast Kansas. The surface ridge sliding eastward on Saturday, \nalong with modest pressure falls across the High Plains, should \nsupport the return of southerly low level flow as soon as Saturday \nafternoon. Temperatures will gradually rebound this weekend with \nhighs on Saturday and Sunday in the 60s. Overnight lows in the mid \n30s Saturday night could be just cold enough for areas of frost once \nagain, then overnight lows Sunday night will be a bit more mild with \n40s expected area-wide.\n\n...NEXT WEEK...\n\nDeterministic and ensemble guidance next week signals a rather \nstagnant mid/upper level pattern. Persistent deep layer troughing is \nanticipated across the western CONUS, and mid/upper ridging will \nremain stuck over the central and southern plains. With persistent \nsouthwesterly upper flow over the southern Rockies, a standing \nsurface trough will remain across the High Plains and encourage \nsteady southerly low-level flow. This pattern supports a high \nlikelihood that temperatures will rebound quickly next week, and \nhighs could return to the 80s as soon as Wednesday. These above \naverage highs should continue through the end of next week. In \ngeneral, moisture looks to hold off over the next 6-7 days. Long \nrange global models are hinting at the possibility that moisture \nfrom another tropical cyclone off the west coast of Mexico could be \npulled northward into the southern and central plains next weekend. \nThat being said, there are still a number a details that still need \nto be ironed out between now and then.",
